artifact.self_contained_problem:
Work in the model-theoretic category of groups of finite Morley rank. Recall that a group of finite Morley rank is a group equipped with a structure in which every definable set has a well-defined finite ordinal-valued Morley rank, and a "definable" subset/subgroup means one defined by a first-order formula with parameters. A group is called connected if it has no proper definable subgroup of finite index.

Let S and M be groups of finite Morley rank. Assume:
(i) S is infinite and simple;
(ii) every proper definable subgroup of S is nilpotent;
(iii) M is non-trivial and connected, and acts on S definably and faithfully (so M embeds definably into the group of definable automorphisms of S);
(iv) the action of M on S is irreducible, meaning that M leaves invariant no definable subgroup of S other than {1} and S itself.

Prove that under these hypotheses the action of M on S is equivalent to the action of S on itself by inner automorphisms (conjugation); equivalently, there is a definable isomorphism between M and S (or its image in Inn(S) = S) that intertwines the given action of M on S with the conjugation action of S on S.

